WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.160
if we want to have a conversation about things
that could not be for profit.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.160
[inaudible]

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.080
here is obviously on this list and we've been
talking about that for years and that seems

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.280
to often be the one that is the most obvious
to the largest number of people.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.420
Although there's still some debate about that
in some political circles.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.270
We can talk about whether education should
be for-profit.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:25.990
We could talk about whether student lending
should be for profit or certain types of housing

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.260
or whatever else.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:33.250
But increasingly we should be talking about
the Internet, which at this point is really

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.320
just a utility which underlies almost everything
we do at this point.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.190
And I'm going to probably end up repeating
this again, but to be a full participant in

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:46.700
2019 society and economy, you effectively
need access to the Internet and probably high

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.200
speed Internet at that.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.610
And there are individual towns that have done
what's called municipal broadband, but the

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:58.260
reason that they've done this is that usually
those towns are too small for the big Internet

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:04.170
service providers to want to come in and set
up shop and be the monopoly provider in those

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.680
municipalities.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.990
And so I was thrilled to see that 2020 Democratic
presidential candidate and Massachusetts Senator

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:17.670
Elizabeth Warren put out our plan yesterday
for an Internet public option along with her

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:18.670
plan.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.400
By the way, to bring back net neutrality,
and as we've talked about with Medicare for

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.030
all a public option is better than nothing.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.310
It may not be the ideal.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:28.880
And with Internet, it's probably similar,
but I still want to deal with this in detail

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.000
today because it could be a really big improvement
over what we have.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.490
And here is Elizabeth Warren's idea.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:42.040
You pass a federal law to prevent states from
restricting towns that want to do public broadband.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.310
That's number one.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:50.479
Number two, you do a federal grant for $85
billion, which will be set up to cover 90%

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:58.440
of the costs for utility co-ops, nonprofits,
cities and counties that want to lay fiber

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.380
for their own broadband networks.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.630
The federal government will subsidize 90%
of it.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:09.910
So the city town co-op, whatever, only has
to pay for 10% of it for profit entities would

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:15.320
not be eligible for that grant, which will
push a lot of internet service over to nonprofits

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.700
and Co ops and municipalities, which would
be great to qualify for the money.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.740
Uh, the organization that is looking to do
this is going to have to agree to provide

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:31.730
at least one affordable plan for low income
people, a special low price to increase access

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.819
to internet, particularly for low income people.

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:39.360
And Meanwhile, yeah, Elizabeth Warren would
also restore net neutrality.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.590
She would appoint commissioners, which would
regulate the internet as a common carrier.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.550
That's a huge, huge thing.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:49.450
So the breaking news here, which is not breaking
news to lots of people in my audience, is

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.190
that both sides aren't the same on many issues
when they are.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:03:00.180
I tell you, I've been crystal clear that too
many issues have Republicans and Democrats

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.610
basically working together to maintain the
status quo and to reinforce that status quo.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:10.820
When Democrats require criticism, I'm their
criticizing them, but on lots of important

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.030
issues, the two parties are not the same.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.190
One issue we've talked about already, and
could it ever be more clear than now under

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:24.160
Donald Trump rather than Hillary Clinton choosing
judges in the United States Supreme Court

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.460
justices and lower level judges.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.450
As we talked about on the bonus show this
week, Donald Trump's power to do that has

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.130
had such a significant impact that will be
long lasting.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:40.840
Donald Trump's judicial choices, more sort
of a celebrated in the Supreme Court, but

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.400
also in lower level courts as we've talked
about.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.000
We'll have an effect on the judiciary of the
United States for decades to come.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.480
Hillary Clinton would have been very different
on that.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.940
You might not like her position on whatever,
but Democrats and Republicans drastically

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.270
different on that.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:04:00.480
It's very similar with the Internet regulation
and net neutrality.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:07.300
In 2018 all democrats and three Republicans
voted to restore net neutrality.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.300
Okay.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:15.660
In the house a few months ago, 231 democrats
and one Republican in the House voted to restore

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:21.009
net neutrality and then Mitch McConnell, Republican
Senate majority leader blocked it from even

00:04:21.009 --> 00:04:23.240
coming up in the Senate at all.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.409
So we need to support candidates with the
right views on this issue.

00:04:27.409 --> 00:04:29.840
I love that Elizabeth Warren, I've made no
secret.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.770
I think it's great that she's been running
one of the most substantive campaigns of 2020

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.589
so far, slowly but surely creeping up in the
polls.

00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:41.919
And she did it again in a couple of recent
polls, which I don't have time to talk about

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:44.509
today, but maybe we'll deal with on Monday.

00:04:44.509 --> 00:04:48.870
She has been doing that with substantive policy
proposals.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:53.930
No cheap shots, no, you know, debate, kill,
shot attempts the way some other candidates

00:04:53.930 --> 00:05:00.270
have been doing just policy, detailed policy,
which she's increasingly good at explaining

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.810
in clear and direct terms.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:03.210
And that's a really good thing.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:11.080
So bottom line in 2019, you essentially need
Internet and, and I would argue not just internet

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:17.360
but high speed Internet to fully participate
in society and in the economy period flat-out.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.729
Thus it should be a public utility.

00:05:20.729 --> 00:05:23.389
Anything short of that falls short of the
goal.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:28.789
But these public option, uh, programs like
what Elizabeth Warren is suggesting here are

00:05:28.789 --> 00:05:30.539
absolutely great ideas.

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:33.530
I think that legally they are quite simple.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:37.749
So when Warren talks about, oh, I want to
pass a wealth tax, uh, okay, you could like

00:05:37.749 --> 00:05:41.940
the idea, not like the idea, think it's the
right way or the wrong way to reduce inequality,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:47.940
think that inequality is an issue, but legally
it will be very complex to get a wealth tax

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:48.940
pass.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.039
That's just the reality.

00:05:50.039 --> 00:05:54.999
This public option Internet plan and the re
and statement of net neutrality would not

00:05:54.999 --> 00:05:56.490
be legally complicated.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.860
It can be complicated in the terms of lobbyists,
in terms of lobbyist opposition and massive

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:05.979
ad dollars lobbying, just average people to
be against it and massive lobbying dollars

00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:08.030
lobbying politicians to be against it.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:12.830
That will be complicated, but when it comes
to the legality of doing it, there are not

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:16.680
constitutional issues that are not commerce
issues or any other issues that I am aware

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:17.680
of.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.719
It's merely the political will and that's
why this is absolutely fantastic.

